        "content": "Based on the GitHub repository page, here's what Zaivern Code offers:\n\n**Launch Date:** Created July 20, 2026 (according to the GitHub metadata)\n\n**What Zaivern Code Does:**\n\nZaivern Code functions as an \"AI cockpit editor\" that lets developers operate multiple AI coding tools from a single interface. Unlike the AI assistants themselves, this application serves as a centralized control panel for managing them simultaneously.\n\nKey capabilities for developers include:\n\n- **Unified Agent Cockpit**: Display multiple AI coding tools in a tile layout so you can monitor their status at a glance. It includes startup presets for 29 different AI tools.\n\n- **Broadcast Commands**: Send the same instruction to all active AI agents from one input field, eliminating the need to copy-paste across multiple terminal windows.\n\n- **Approval Management**: Track when AIs await permission for operations and handle approvals with one-click actions.\n\n- **Fleet Monitoring**: View a unified status dashboard showing which agents are thinking, editing, executing, or awaiting confirmation.\n\n- **Mobile Remote Control**: Monitor progress, issue instructions, approve actions, and edit files from your smartphone.\n\n- **Built-in Code Editor**: Read code and review AI-made changes directly within the application. Supports images, PDFs, CSV, and Markdown files.\n\nThe tool runs on macOS, Windows, and Linux. It requires separate installation of the actual AI tools you want to manage (such as Claude Code, Codex, or Gemini CLI)—it doesn't include these tools itself.\n\nZaivern Code is distributed under the Apache-2.0 license as open source software."
