        "content": "     1→# Cloudflare Computer\n     2→\n     3→Cloudflare Computer is a virtual filesystem that lives inside a\n     4→Durable Object. The Durable Object holds the authoritative state in\n     5→SQLite and exposes one pluggable execution surface through\n     6→`workspace.runtime`. Three backends ship today:\n     7→\n     8→- **Container** projects the SQLite state into a sandbox container as\n     9→  a real FUSE mount. A sandbox-side daemon (`computerd`) mounts the state\n    10→  as a filesystem and syncs changes back over a capnweb RPC channel.\n    11→  Full Linux userland, real binaries, real network.\n    12→- **Isolate shell** runs [just-bash](https://github.com/vercel-labs/just-bash)\n    13→  in a Dynamic Worker. It reaches the authoritative Workspace over\n    14→  Workers RPC, so there is no second store or sync round trip.\n    15→- **Isolate JavaScript** runs an ECMAScript module in a fresh Dynamic\n    16→  Worker with structured input/results, durable relative imports,\n    17→  configured libraries, Workspace-backed `node:fs/promises`, and trusted `ws:git` and\n    18→  `ws:artifacts` modules.\n    19→\n    20→A Workspace may register multiple backends under stable IDs.\n    21→`workspace.runtime.exec(source, { backend })` is the single execution\n    22→entry point; the selected backend defines whether `source` is a shell\n    23→command or an ECMAScript module. Backends connect lazily on first use.\n    24→\n    25→Workspace can also be constructed without a backend at all, giving\n    26→callers the filesystem on its own.\n    27→\n    28→> [!IMPORTANT]\n    29→> **PREVIEW ONLY** This package is provided as a preview for feedback only.\n    30→> APIs are unstable and the design is subject to change.\n    31→>\n    32→> Suitable for experiments, exploration and prototypes. It is NOT suitable\n    33→> for production use at this time.\n    34→>\n    35→> The specification under [`docs/`](docs/) is forward-looking — read it for\n    36→> intent, not as description of the code today.\n    37→\n    38→## Using it\n    39→\n    40→If you want to build on Cloudflare Computer, install\n    41→[`@cloudflare/computer`](packages/computer/README.md) and follow that\n    42→package's README — it has the installation steps, the entrypoint map,\n    43→and worked examples of the `fs` and `runtime` surfaces.\n    44→\n    45→To contribute feedback, see [`CONTRIBUTING.md`](CONTRIBUTING.md).\n    46→Approved collaborators should follow [`COLLABORATORS.md`](COLLABORATORS.md)\n    47→for setup, build, and test instructions.\n    48→\n    49→## Examples\n    50→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
